<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rock of Dunamase</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rock of Dunamase is a limestone outcrop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-laois">County Laois</a>, Ireland, crowned by the ruins of a castle. A fortress there was plundered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> in 845 and later held by the kings of Leinster. After the Anglo-Norman invasion it passed by marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> and then to the Marshal family, who built the stone castle; it was in ruins by the 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rock-of-dunamase.jpg" alt="Rock of Dunamase" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kent Clemmons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_of_Dunamase.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
